 "text": " What if you melted all the gold ever mined into one cube? In all of history, we've dug up about 222,000 tonnes of gold, two thirds of it since 1950. Melt it down, pour it into one block, and the cube is only 22.5 metres on each side, about as tall as a seven-storey building. Small, but heavy. It weighs as much as 22 Eiffel Towers. At today's price, it's worth about 25 trillion dollars. Now share it out. Every person on Earth gets 27 grams. A little lump worth about 3,000 dollars. Want more? There's gold in the sea. In the 1920s, the chemist Fritz Haber tried to pull it out of seawater to pay Germany's war debts. Then he measured. There was about a thousand times less gold than he'd hoped, he gave up. So all the gold we have still fits in one cube. Subscribe for more What Ifs.",
